# Vuex 4 — Legacy Architecture (Educational Only)

> ⚠️ **Isolation rule:** the snippets below are *reading material*. This app
> does **not** install, import, or run Vuex anywhere. Runnable artifacts live
> only under `content/tutorials/vue/state-management/vuex/` as text.

## The classic shape

```js
// store/index.js — Vue 3 + Vuex 4
import { createStore } from 'vuex'

export default createStore({
  state: () => ({ count: 0 }),
  getters: {
    doubled: (state) => state.count * 2
  },
  mutations: {
    INCREMENT(state) { state.count += 1 },          // sync only!
    SET_COUNT(state, value) { state.count = value }
  },
  actions: {
    async fetchCount({ commit }) {
      const value = await api.count()
      commit('SET_COUNT', value)                    // actions commit mutations
    }
  }
})

// Components
computed: { ...mapGetters(['doubled']) },
methods: { ...mapActions(['fetchCount']) }
```

## Why did mutations exist?

Pre-Vue-3 reactivity could not reliably track who changed what. Forcing every
change through named, synchronous `mutations` gave devtools a replayable log —
an audit trail bolted onto application code.

Vue 3's proxy-based reactivity made that unnecessary: Pinia's devtools trace
arbitrary changes natively.

## Modules & namespacing

```js
modules: {
  cart: { namespaced: true, state, getters, mutations, actions }
}
// dispatch('cart/addItem', item)
commit('cart/INCREMENT', null, { root: false })
```

Namespacing strings (`'cart/addItem'`) are typed nowhere — a large refactor
surface TypeScript cannot check.

## When you'll meet Vuex
Maintaining pre-2021 codebases. You now can read it fluently and argue its
successor's design from experience rather than fashion.

examples.code:
// Educational artifact ONLY — never imported by this app.
const state = () => ({ items: [] })

const getters = {
  total: (state) => state.items.reduce((s, i) => s + i.price, 0)
}

const mutations = {
  ADD_ITEM(state, item) { state.items.push(item) },
  CLEAR(state) { state.items = [] }
}

const actions = {
  addItem({ commit }, item) {
    if (!item || typeof item.price !== 'number') return
    commit('ADD_ITEM', item)
  }
}

export default { namespaced: true, state, getters, mutations, actions }

exercise.starterCode:
actions: {
  async rename({ state }, name) {
    state.name = name        // ← which rule does this break?
    await api.save(name)
  }
}

exercise.solution:
mutations: {
  RENAME(state, name) { state.name = name }
},
actions: {
  async rename({ commit }, name) {
    commit('RENAME', name)
    await api.save(name)
  }
}
